#{[#edb/ref 17592186049360 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097737} :activity/id #uuid "886871c8-0b6e-56ce-bc7f-54affec4fd88" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097737 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097833 :tutorial/content "A **unit vector** is a vector whose magnitude is equal to $1$.\n\nGiven any vector $\\mathbf a$, we can create a unit vector $\\mathbf u$ by dividing $\\mathbf a$ by its magnitude $\\mid \\, \\mathbf{a} \\, \\mid$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid}\n$$\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathbf{a}\n$$\n\n![](images/c7/c78d5b75c1d5928e7b269a4a67229487b3d3324e9815b11c5fd1f47f63437d82.png)" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097738}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097738 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096813 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096447 :question/id #uuid "2a61b115-4e26-5966-b2a9-d2fedef6b728" :question/math-academy-id "e-4282" :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ has magnitude $3$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$." :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$, we divide $\\mathbf{a}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\mathbf{a}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097739}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097739 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096814 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096493 :question/id #uuid "6dfe09f0-30e2-53bc-a8f6-d7e4a3b8232a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5181" :question/problem "![](images/d3/d3042242136b82b867b8bd7911761ee38318924df0c9979488d20f04117c45d9.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf a$." :question/worked-solution "![](images/b3/b316448c885a20a7ed60871ce0f1a4dc4f77bd4658dc6eda3407fb3c89a088cb.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have\n\n$$\n\\angle AHB = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\n, $AH=3$ and $BH=2$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^{2} + BH^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3^{2} + 2^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9 + 4} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{13}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$ is $\\sqrt{13}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{\\mid a \\mid} \\cdot a \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector in a Diagram"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097740}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097740 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096815 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096448 :question/id #uuid "23d9a601-974b-51c2-a6ea-0ebadde19efc" :question/math-academy-id "e-4283" :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ is directed horizontally to the right and has magnitude $10$, while the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is directed vertically down and has magnitude $20$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a+b}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $\\mathbf{a+b}$. According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$ goes from the tail of $\\mathbf{a}$ to the head of $\\mathbf{b}$:\n\n![](images/32/32c48d8c19fc68fbc172b240ded922f3e2ebc12804411c318ff63fe999aaedcc.png)\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $\\mathbf{a+b}$, we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{a + b}{\\mid a + b \\mid}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, first notice that we obtain a right triangle. So, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}a + b & ^{2} &= & a & ^{2} +\\left.\\end{vmatrix}b\\right|^{2} \\\\\n&= 10^{2} + 20^{2} \\\\\n&= 100 + 400 \\\\\n&= 500\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mathbf{a+b} &= \\sqrt{500}=10\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{a + b}{\\mid a + b \\mid} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10\\sqrt{5}}(a + b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Unit Vector Parallel to a Vector Sum"}}] :activity/title "Unit Vectors" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096884}] [#edb/ref 17592186049335 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106925} :activity/id #uuid "d489d7ad-a521-5b24-bbbd-5997d33ba8e3" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106925 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107078 :tutorial/content "Geometrically, the absolute value of the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})$ represents the volume enclosed by the parallelepiped whose sides are formed by the three vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nTo illustrate, consider the parallelepiped shown below. The sides of this parallelepiped are formed by the vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\n![](images/25/2597b48a010607fca71c551dbce8e9e2bbeda47d0892a582347ae1cdf19f1bf6.png)\n\nThe volume of this parallelepiped is given by the absolute value of the triple product:\n\n$$\nV = |\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})|\n$$\n\nA proof of this result will be given at the end of this lesson." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106926}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106926 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105907 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105596 :question/id #uuid "b3be2b13-b455-5dde-accf-259c075e1e1f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4350" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 1,-4,2 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 0,-2,1 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle -1,0,3 \\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|-(-4)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 0)+4(0\\cdot 3-1\\cdot (-1))+2(0\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =-6+4-4 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV = |-6| = 6.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Volume of a Parallelepiped Spanned by Three Vectors"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106927}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106927 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107079 :tutorial/content "Three vectors are said to be **coplanar** if they are parallel to the same plane.\n\nWhen we have three coplanar vectors, the parallelepiped they span is *flat* on the plane, and therefore its volume is zero. Consequently, the triple product of the vectors is zero as well.\n\nSo, if three vectors are coplanar, then their triple product is zero. The converse is also true: if the triple product of three nonzero vectors is zero, then the vectors are coplanar.\n\nTherefore, we have the following statement:\n\n> $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ are coplanar $\\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad$ $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = 0$" :tutorial/title "The Volume Spanned by Coplanar Vectors"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106928}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106928 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105908 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105597 :question/id #uuid "c6f2dacc-11b0-5689-97d9-e1869c64aae1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4351" :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle k,2,1 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle -2,-1,1 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle -1,1,2 \\rangle$ are coplanar." :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}k & 2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =k\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =k(-1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1)-2(-2\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1)+(-2\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =-3k+6-3 \\\\  & =-3k+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ -3k+3 & =0 \\\\ k & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=1$."} :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Three Vectors are Coplanar"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106929}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106929 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107085 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we are not given the vectors that represent the sides of a parallelepiped. Instead, we might be given the points at which the vertices lie.\n\nTo compute the volume of a parallelepiped given the vertices, we can still use a similar process as before. The only difference is that we have to start by computing the displacement vectors between the points.\n\nOnce we have the displacement vectors, then we can find the desired volume by computing the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nLet's see an example of this." :tutorial/title "Calculating the Volume of a Parallelepiped Given the Coordinates of the Vertices"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106930}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106930 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105909 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105598 :question/id #uuid "4deee6cc-69e4-5da4-9c78-57bf6c988500" :question/math-academy-id "e-4352" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overrightarrow{AB},$ $\\overrightarrow{AC},$ and $\\overrightarrow{AD},$ given the points $A(3,-1,2),$ $B(2,1,1),$ $C(-1,2,1),$ and $D(4,4,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overrightarrow{AB},$ $\\overrightarrow{AC},$ and $\\overrightarrow{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(2i+j+k)-(3i-j+2k) \\\\  & =-i+2j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-i+2j+k)-(3i-j+2k) \\\\  & =-4i+3j-k \\\\  & =⟨-4,3,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(4i+4j+2k)-(3i-j+2k) \\\\  & =i+5j \\\\  & =⟨1,5,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(3\\cdot 0-5\\cdot (-1))-2((-4)\\cdot 0-1\\cdot (-1))-((-4)\\cdot 5-1\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =-5-2+23 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overrightarrow{AB},$ $\\overrightarrow{AC},$ and $\\overrightarrow{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|16|=16.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Volume of a Parallelepiped Spanned by Three Position Vectors Given Their Coordinates"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106931}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106931 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107084 :tutorial/content "We have been using the fact that the volume of a parallelepiped spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ is given by the absolute value of the triple product:\n\n$$\nV =  |\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})|\n$$\n\nBut why is this formula true? To understand why, remember that the volume of a parallelepiped can be obtained by multiplying the area of the base by the height.\n\nAs we can see in the diagram below, \n\n- the area of the base is given by $A = | \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} |,$ and\n\n- the height is given by $h = |\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c}}\\:\\mathbf{a}|.$\n\n![](images/cf/cfc492127bed6d6929518a708288691c5fe79e1842be6ecf92666bed6b03200b.png)\n\nTo compute the volume, we will multiply the area of the base by the height. First, though, let's write down the expression for the height:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =|{\\text{proj}}_{b\\times c}\\,a| \\\\  & =|\\frac{a\\cdot (b\\times c)}{|b\\times c|}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|a\\cdot (b\\times c)|}{|b\\times c|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, multiplying the area of the base by the height, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =A\\cdot h \\\\  & =|b\\times c|\\cdot \\frac{|a\\cdot (b\\times c)|}{|b\\times c|} \\\\  & =|a\\cdot (b\\times c)|.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Proof That the Volume of a Parallelepiped is Given by the Absolute Value of the Triple Product"}}] :activity/title "Volumes of Parallelepipeds" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105964}] [#edb/ref 17592186049310 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111678} :activity/id #uuid "f14be6fb-7490-5e50-9e2d-7ccd72daa91c" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111678 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111814 :tutorial/content "Some linear equations have **infinitely many solutions.** \n\nFor example, consider the following equation: \n\n$$\nx+5 = x + 5\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to solve this equation by subtracting $x$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =x+5 \\\\ x+5-x & =x+5-x \\\\ 5 & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, what does $x$ equal? The statement $5=5$ is *always* true, irrespective of the value of $x.$ This tells us that *any* value of $x$ is a solution to the equation!\n\nDon't believe it? If we substitute any value, say $x=3,$ into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =x+5 \\\\ 3+5 & =3+5 \\\\ 8 & =8.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's important to note that substituting *any* value for $x$ into the equation will lead to a true statement. For example, the values \n\n$$\nx=42,\\qquad x=-\\dfrac 1 4, \\qquad x=1.2345\n$$\n\nare *all* solutions to the original equation.\n\nTo summarize:\n\n- Our original equation has *infinitely many solutions.*\n\n- The solution of the equation is \"*any (real) value of* $x$\"." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111679}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111679 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110719 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110372 :question/id #uuid "6355ac1e-bb4d-5133-9917-04dfb2bc5a0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-18854" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\n- $4x + 1 = 4x + 1$\n\n- $-3x = -4x + 6$\n\n- $2x + 7 = 2x - 1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+1 & =4x+1 \\\\ 4x+1-4x & =4x+1-4x \\\\ 1 & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $4x + 1 = 4x + 1$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x & =-4x+6 \\\\ -3x+4x & =-4x+6+4x \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $-3x = -4x + 6$ has the unique solution $x = 6.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+7 & =2x-1 \\\\ 2x+7-2x & =2x-1-2x \\\\ 7 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111680}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111680 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111818 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = bx,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a = b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nFor example, each of the following equations has infinitely many solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x=2x,\\,\\frac{1}{3}x=\\frac{1}{3}x\\,-2.16x=-2.16x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo show that the general equation $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b,$ we can substitute $a$ for $b$ in our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax & =bx \\\\ ax & =(a)x \\\\ ax & =ax\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify this equation by subtracting $ax$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax & =ax \\\\ ax-ax & =ax-ax \\\\ 0 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation, $0=0,$ is always true, regardless of the value of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b,$ and any value of $x$ is a solution to the equation." :tutorial/title "Conditions for an Equation To Have Infinitely Many Solutions"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111681}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111681 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111815 :tutorial/content "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n2qx = 10x,\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. If we're given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, how can we find the value of $q?$\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = bx{:}$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2q}_{\\large a} \\ x = \\underbrace{10}_{\\large b} \\ x\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n$$\na = 2q, \\qquad b = 10.\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b.$ Therefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n2q = 10.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2q & =10 \\\\ \\frac{2q}{2} & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\ q & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2qx = 10x$ has infinitely many solutions if $q = 5.$\n\nWe can check that this answer is correct by substituting $q = 5$ into our original equation. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2qx & =10x \\\\ 2(5)x & =10x \\\\ 10x & =10x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand simplifying this equation by subtracting $10x$ from both sides yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x & =10x \\\\ 10x-10x & =10x-10x \\\\ 0 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is true for all values of $x.$\n\n**Note:** The constant $q$ in this problem is sometimes called a **parameter.**" :tutorial/title "Problems Involving Unknown Parameters"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111682}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111682 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110720 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110368 :question/id #uuid "4a8f8deb-2ff1-56d2-888a-3e51eb805bc4" :question/math-academy-id "e-17212" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(5s + 4)x = 9x\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $s?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a = b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na = 5s + 4, \\qquad b = 9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n5s + 4 = 9.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5s & =5 \\\\ s & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111683}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111683 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111819 :tutorial/content "Each of the following equations has infinitely many solutions:\n\n$$\n2x + 1 = 2x + 1, \\qquad \\dfrac13x -2 = \\dfrac13x -2, \\qquad 1-x = 1 - x\n$$\n\nMore generally, suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax + b = cx + d,\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b, c,$ and $d$ are constants. Then, this equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na = c\\quad \\text{and}\\quad b = d.\n$$\n\nNote that if we substitute $c=a$ and $d=b$ into this equation, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax+b & =cx+d \\\\ ax+b & =(a)x+(b) \\\\ ax+b & =ax+b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation by subtracting $ax$ from both sides of the equation gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax+b & =ax+b \\\\ ax+b-ax & =ax+b-ax \\\\ b & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation, $b = b,$ is always true. Therefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions, and any real value of $x$ is a solution." :tutorial/title "Conditions for a General Linear Equation To Have Infinitely Many Solutions"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111684}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111684 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111817 :tutorial/content "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(s - 2)x + t = 6x + 9,\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ and $t$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, how can we find the values of $s$ and $t?$\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax+b = cx+d{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(s-2)}_{\\large a} \\ x + \\underbrace{t}_{\\large b} = \\underbrace{6}_{\\large c} \\ x + \\underbrace{9}_{\\large d}\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax+b = cx+d$ has infinitely many solutions when $a=c$ and $b=d.$\n\nSo, to determine the values of $s$ and $t$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n({\\color{blue}{{s - 2}}})x + {\\color{red}{{t}}} = {\\color{blue}{{6}}}x + {\\color{red}{{9}}}\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we **equate the coefficients** of $x,$ and we **equate the constants:**\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{{s - 2}}} = {\\color{blue}{{6}}}.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{{t}}} = {\\color{red}{{9}}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $s = 8$ and $t=9,$ our original equation has infinitely many solutions." :tutorial/title "Problems Involving Unknown Parameters in General Linear Equations"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111685}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111685 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110721 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110371 :question/id #uuid "6eeec12a-5da8-5113-916a-58f4ca807207" :question/math-academy-id "e-18852" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(k+4)x + 3r = 6x + 12\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ and $r$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k+r?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax + b = cx + d\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b, c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na = c\\quad \\text{and}\\quad b = d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $k$ and $r$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(k+4)x + 3r = 6x + 12\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\nk + 4 = 6.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n3r = 12.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k = 2$ and $r = 4,$ we have\n\n$$\nk + r = 2+4 = 6.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving a General Equation With Infinitely Many Solutions For Unknown Parameters"}}] :activity/title "Linear Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110810}] [#edb/ref 17592186049255 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125528} :activity/id #uuid "76681192-0bc8-5fd9-98fc-5fd254bc93fd" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125318 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124469 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124276 :question/id #uuid "253b765d-be85-574e-8e32-c359ffbecdea" :question/math-academy-id "e-3694" :question/problem "If $f(x,y) = x^2 + 2 y^3$ and $\\mathbf r (t) = \\sin t \\ \\mathbf i + e^{t} \\ \\mathbf j,$ compute $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{3})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+6{y}^{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x = \\sin t$ and $y=e^{t}$  into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+6({e}^{t})^{2}\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6{e}^{2t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\text{ }i+6{e}^{2t}\\text{ }j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\text{ }i+{e}^{t}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6{e}^{2t}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+6{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Two Variables"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125320}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125319 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125749 :tutorial/content "We can consider $f(\\mathbf{r}(t))$ as the values taken by the function $f$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t).$ So, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))]\n$$\n\ncan be interpreted as the **rate of change of $f$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t).$**\n\nFurthermore, if $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is a level curve $f(x,y)=c,$ then by the chain rule we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =c \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(f(r(t))) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(c) \\\\ ∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{'}(t) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom this, we can conclude that the gradient vector $\\nabla f$ is orthogonal to every level curve of $f.$" :tutorial/title "The Derivative as a Rate of Change"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125321}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125320 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124470 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124277 :question/id #uuid "033e459e-5148-5e78-ab5c-7e455b06cd64" :question/math-academy-id "e-3695" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z) = xz\\ln{y}$ and $\\mathbf r (t) =  t^2 \\, \\mathbf i + t  \\, \\mathbf j +t^3  \\, \\mathbf k,$ compute $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,k \\\\  & =z\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,i+\\frac{xz}{y}\\,j+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x= t^2,$ $y = t$ and $z= t^3$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & ={t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+{t}^{4}\\,j+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,k \\\\  & =2t\\,i+\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+{t}^{4}\\,j+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,k)\\cdot (2t\\,i+\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\cdot 2t+({t}^{4})\\cdot 1+({t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\cdot 3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2{t}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{t}^{4}+3{t}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\  & ={t}^{4}(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Three Variables"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125529}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125321 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124471 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124278 :question/id #uuid "f44b6ea6-694c-5f54-94ee-4c4a699bf4eb" :question/math-academy-id "e-3696" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=x^2-y^2-z$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf r (t) = \\left \\langle \\sin t, \\cos t, t  \\right \\rangle$ at the point $t=\\dfrac \\pi 4.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf r (t)$ is given by $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] .$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-z),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-z),\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-z)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x,-2y,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x = \\sin t, y=\\cos t,$ and $z=t$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1⟩ \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))] & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-1 \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Rate of Change of a Multivariable Function at a Point Using the Chain Rule"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125530}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125528 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125835 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the function \n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{r}) = f(x,y) = xy^2,\n$$\n\nwhich takes a vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle x(t), y(t) \\right\\rangle = \\left\\langle \\cos t, \\sin t \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nas input. How do we calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}t}\\left[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\right]?$\n\nTo do this, we use the **multivariable chain rule in vector form**, which states\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}t}\\left[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\right] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now apply this to our functions. First, we compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(f(x,y)),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(f(x,y))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{y}^{2},2xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\cos t$ and $y=\\sin t$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨(\\operatorname{sin}t)^{2},2(\\operatorname{cos}t)(\\operatorname{sin}t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{sin}^{2}t,2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}t,\\operatorname{cos}t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =⟨{sin}^{2}t,2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}t,\\operatorname{cos}t⟩ \\\\  & =-{sin}^{3}t+2\\operatorname{sin}t{cos}^{2}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125318}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125529 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124593 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124404 :question/id #uuid "c623bb85-401d-5d00-b4a8-cb86570d3304" :question/math-academy-id "e-8282" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}[f(\\mathbf r(t))]$ at $t = \\pi$ given that\n$\\nabla f(x,y) = (2y - 1) \\, \\mathbf i + (2x-1) \\, \\mathbf j$\nand\n$\\mathbf r (t) = \\sin{t} \\, \\mathbf i + \\cos{t} \\, \\mathbf j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = (2y - 1) \\, \\mathbf i + (2x - 1) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x = \\sin{t}$ and $y = \\cos{t}$ into $\\nabla f(x,y)$ to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\,i+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1\\,i+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =2({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t = \\pi,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\pi ))] & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =2(1)+0-(-1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Derivative at a Point Using the Chain Rule"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125319}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125530 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125836 :tutorial/content "The chain rule for a function $f(\\mathbf{r}),$ where $\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle x_1(t), x_2(t), \\ldots, x_m(t) \\right\\rangle$ is a vector-valued function of $m$ variables, is the same as before:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t)\n$$\n\nHow does this chain rule in vector form relate to the chain rule we have seen before, which states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d f} {\\textrm d t} \t= \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x_1} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm d x_1}{\\textrm d t}  \n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x_2} \\cdot  \\dfrac {\\textrm d x_2}{\\textrm d t} + \\dots + \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x_m} \\cdot  \\dfrac {\\textrm d x_m}{\\textrm d t}?\n$$\n\nLet's expand $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t)$ to see. Recall that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t))=⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}},\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}},…,\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{'}(t)=⟨\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{1}}{dt},\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{2}}{dt},…,\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{m}}{dt}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}},\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}},…,\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m}}⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{1}}{dt},\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{2}}{dt},…,\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{m}}{dt}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{1}}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{2}}{\\text{d}t}+⋯+\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{m}}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThey are exactly the same, just different notations!\n\n**Note**: If we replace $\\mathbf{r}$ with a single-valued function $g(t),$ we get back the chain rule from single-variable calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(g(t))] & =∇f(g(t))\\cdot {g}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Intuition Behind the Vector Form of the Multivariable Chain Rule"}}] :activity/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule in Vector Form" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124728}]}
